# walk-in DHCP — the SECOND scope, on the walk-in leg (eth1).
#
# RENDERED, NOT EDITED. scripts/retronet/walkin-net/provision-walkin-net.sh
# substitutes the @NAME@ placeholders and installs the result in CT 951 as
# /etc/retronet/walkin-dhcp.env. Hand-edit it there and the next provision
# overwrites you. As-built: docs/lab/walkin/NETWORK-PLANE.md.
#
# It runs the SAME program as the retronet scope (/opt/retronet-dhcp/dhcp.py) as
# a second unit with its own config — not a fork of it. The two differ in
# exactly three ways: the leg they are pinned to, the pool they hand out, and
# how long a lease lives.

# Both scopes must receive the limited broadcast a client with no address sends
# (destined 255.255.255.255), so both bind 0.0.0.0:67 — and that is why
# RN_DHCP_BIND_DEVICE below is not optional. A broadcast is delivered to EVERY
# matching socket, so an unpinned retronet scope would also answer a walk-in
# clone's DISCOVER and burn a 10.99.0.0/24 pool address on every clone that ever
# boots. Pinning each scope to its own leg is what makes them two scopes rather
# than two servers fighting.
RN_DHCP_LISTEN=@RN_DHCP_LISTEN@
RN_DHCP_BIND_DEVICE=@RN_DHCP_BIND_DEVICE@

# This scope's server identifier — the gateway's walk-in address.
RN_DHCP_SERVER_ID=@RN_DHCP_SERVER_ID@
RN_DHCP_SUBNET_MASK=@RN_DHCP_SUBNET_MASK@

# DNS handed to clones: the walk-in leg's own wildcard resolver, so every name a
# clone types resolves to the walk-in origin and never to the retronet's.
RN_DHCP_DNS=@RN_DHCP_DNS@
RN_DHCP_DOMAIN=@RN_DHCP_DOMAIN@

# The clone pool. No reservations: a walk-in clone is anonymous and ephemeral,
# and the retronet's reserved-static convention (a station keeps ONE address so
# exec-over-bridge has a stable target) has no meaning here.
RN_DHCP_POOL=@RN_DHCP_POOL@
RN_DHCP_RESERVATIONS=

# SHORT leases. A clone lives for one session and its address must come back to
# the pool quickly — an hour-long lease on a 100-address pool would strand the
# plane after a busy afternoon.
RN_DHCP_LEASE=@RN_DHCP_LEASE@

# Withholding option 3 (router) is not configurable and never was: the server
# never sends it. A clone therefore has NO default route, which is the
# addressing-level half of "reaches nothing else".
